<p><strong>A campaign works when it has one offer, one end date and the right clients. Outreach works when it happens automatically and you can see what goes out before it does.</strong></p>
<p><em>The scene below is a typical one, not a single named customer.</em></p>
<h2>Tuesday at 2 pm, three empty chairs</h2>
<p>Weekends are full. Weekdays are quiet. The owner posts an offer on social media, forgets to take it down, and a month later half the clients still ask for the "weekday price" on a Saturday.</p>
<h2>Campaign or outreach?</h2>
<ul><li><strong>A campaign</strong> is a dated offer for a reason: fill quiet days, launch a service, welcome new clients.</li><li><strong>Customer outreach</strong> is the follow-up that goes out by itself: a win-back note, a review request, a reminder to rebook.</li></ul>
<h2>How to run a campaign that pays</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Pick one problem,</strong> such as empty weekday afternoons.</li><li><strong>Make one offer with an end date.</strong></li><li><strong>Send it to the right clients,</strong> not everyone.</li><li><strong>Measure it.</strong> Count the bookings it brought, then decide whether to repeat it.</li></ol>
<h2>How Zennly helps</h2>
<p>Zennly's <a href="/features/campaigns/">promotions</a> let you set up a dated offer. <a href="/features/outreach/">Customer outreach</a> sends win-back messages and review requests automatically by email, with an <strong>approval queue</strong> so you check what goes out. SMS and WhatsApp are available on request. Discounts stay under <a href="/features/discount-management/">discount control</a>, so an offer does not turn into free money for the team.</p>
<h2>A quick fix you can start today</h2>
<ol><li>List the clients who have not visited in 60 days.</li><li>Pick one quiet day and one offer that ends on a date.</li><li>Write the date on every message.</li></ol>
